\begin_layout Subsection
Outlier problems
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Outliers in a dataset tend to attract the attention of the evolving process.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Improvement, period.
 No matter how small or irrelevant an improvement is, it is an improvement.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
tournament selection: Trees with this gene survive over the ones without
 this gene.
 There is no complexity threshold holding these trees back.
 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Many ways to fit outliers.
 Outliers are characterized by being in a sparse space.
 The chance of overfitting is quite high though, fitting a weird pattern
 randomly on a sparse dataset has a high chance of 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Sparse data can easily be fit: Improvement can often be achieved with relatively
 few effort leaving the program core untouched.
 Using only a couple of elemental mathematical operations or an if-condition
 is often enough.
 On one hand, it supports capsulating the program into more parts, however,
 the programs tend to make weird overfitting of outliers instead of improving
 the program.
 The core logic needs to fit much more data samples correctly, requiring
 many nodes to achieve only minor improvements.
 Taking care of all of them requires a lot of fine tuning and structural
 changes.
 If an improvement is found, the changes are often fundamental.
 These improvements are harder to achieve, but the required changes often
 scale similar to the outliers improvements.
 As a result, the improvement per node is often quite similar.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Capsulated changes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
overfitting occurs randomly and often.
 functions can “accidentally” fit a sample, improving the regression error
 by only a few.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Crossover dilemma: Quantity over quality: The evolutionary crossover likes
 to populate a generation with these “genes”
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
they do not cause a lot of feature interaction to the rest of the code.
 making an improvement to the partner likely, even if it is not fundamental.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Small branches are more likely to be passed as a whole each time 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Many small changes can accumulate, leaving the 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Crossover does not have complexity measurement.
 Each node is selected with the same probability, doesn't matter if you
 weight 
\begin_inset Formula $\arctan(x)$
